---

<BaseLayout title="Retatrutide — Scientific Library" description="Evidence-led PeptiBloom monograph for investigational triple GIPR, GLP-1R and GCGR agonist retatrutide, evidence cutoff 3 September 2026.">
  <section class="compound-hero section">
    <div class="site-frame compound-hero__grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Scientific Library · Golden Master v1.0</p>
        <h1>Retatrutide</h1>
        <p class="compound-deck">An investigational peptide engineered to activate GIP, GLP-1 and glucagon receptors. Human trials support substantial glycaemic and weight effects within studied populations and durations; important long-term and practical-use questions remain unresolved.</p>
        <div class="compound-meta" aria-label="Scientific entry metadata">
          <span><strong>Status</strong> Investigational</span>
          <span><strong>Evidence cutoff</strong> 03 Sep 2026</span>
          <span><strong>Also known as</strong> LY3437943</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="compound-note">
        <p class="surface-kicker">Reading note</p>
        <h2>Evidence, not a protocol.</h2>
        <p>This entry describes reviewed research evidence. Trial regimens are not approved dosing, and PeptiBloom does not infer third-party vial preparation, cycles or stacking instructions.</p>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--quiet" aria-labelledby="snapshot-title">
    <div class="site-frame">
      <div class="section-heading reading-column">
        <p class="eyebrow">Evidence snapshot</p>
        <h2 id="snapshot-title">What the reviewed evidence can support.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="evidence-grid">
        {evidence.map(([domain, state]) => <article><p>{domain}</p><strong>{state}</strong></article>)}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="site-frame compound-layout">
    <nav class="compound-toc" aria-label="Retatrutide sections">
      <p class="surface-kicker">On this page</p>
      <a href="#identity">Identity & chemistry</a><a href="#pharmacology">Development & pharmacology</a><a href="#handling">Handling evidence</a><a href="#regimens">Studied regimens</a><a href="#interactions">Combinations & interactions</a><a href="#safety">Safety</a><a href="#interpretation">Interpretation</a><a href="#references">References</a><a href="#recommendation">PeptiBloom interpretation</a>
    </nav>

    <article class="compound-article">
      <section id="identity">
        <p class="eyebrow">A · Identity and chemistry</p><h2>Identity & chemistry</h2>
        <h3>1. Name</h3><p><strong>Retatrutide</strong>, development designation <strong>LY3437943 / LY-3437943</strong>. FDA GSRS substance identifier: <strong>UNII NOP2Y096GV</strong>. A UNII identifies a substance; it does not indicate regulatory approval.</p>
        <h3>2. Other names</h3><p>LY3437943, LY-3437943 and Retatrutide [INN/USAN]. “GLP-3” is sometimes used informally online but is not an appropriate scientific classification.</p>
        <h3>3. Formula</h3><p>FDA GSRS alternative chemical representation for the fully modified neutral molecule: <strong>C221H342N46O68</strong>. Backbone-derived calculations must not be confused with the chemically modified molecule or a particular manufacturer salt/form.</p>
        <h3>4. Molecular weight</h3><p>Approximately <strong>4,731.36 Da</strong> for the fully modified neutral molecule under that GSRS representation.</p>
        <h3>5. Amino-acid sequence</h3><code class="sequence">YAQGTFTSDYSILLDKKAQAAFIEYLLEGGPSSGAPPPS</code><p>This 39-residue natural-letter backbone is not a complete chemical description. Retatrutide includes non-standard residues plus lipidation/linker chemistry and a C-terminal amide.</p>
        <h3>6. Family</h3><p>Investigational multi-receptor metabolic peptide agonist targeting <strong>GIPR, GLP-1R and GCGR</strong>. <span class="evidence-inline">Evidence: Solid.</span></p>
      </section>

      <section id="pharmacology">
        <p class="eyebrow">B · Development and pharmacology</p><h2>Development & pharmacology</h2>
        <h3>7. History</h3><p>Clinical development was initially reported under LY3437943. Peer-reviewed human evidence progressed from phase 1b in 2022 to phase 2 diabetes and obesity studies in 2023, a MASLD liver-fat substudy in 2024, and peer-reviewed phase 3 T2D evidence from TRANSCEND-T2D-1 in 2026. At the evidence cutoff, retatrutide remains investigational.</p>
        <h3>8. Mechanism of action</h3><p>Retatrutide is engineered to activate GIPR, GLP-1R and GCGR within one molecule. Structural and functional work supports engagement of all three receptors. Demonstrating receptor engagement does not establish how much of each clinical effect is attributable to each receptor individually.</p>
        <div class="receptor-row" aria-label="Receptor targets"><span>GIPR</span><span>GLP-1R</span><span>GCGR</span></div>
        <h3>9. Pharmacokinetics</h3><p>The phase 1b human study reported approximately dose-proportional pharmacokinetics and an elimination half-life of roughly <strong>6 days</strong>. This is consistent with the once-weekly <em>investigational study schedule</em>; it is not approved prescribing information.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="handling">
        <p class="eyebrow">C · Handling and preparation evidence</p><h2>Where evidence stops.</h2>
        <div class="gap-card"><h3>10. Stability</h3><p>The reviewed evidence does not establish a universal post-reconstitution stability period for third-party lyophilised retatrutide vials. Stability is formulation-specific.</p></div>
        <div class="gap-card"><h3>11. Conservation / storage</h3><p>No universal storage instruction for arbitrary third-party retatrutide material is authorized by this review. Investigational clinical supply, manufacturer-specific research material and validated pharmaceutical stability data are distinct evidence categories.</p></div>
        <div class="gap-card"><h3>12. Reconstitution</h3><p><strong>Universal validated protocol not established.</strong> The clinical literature does not establish a general bacteriostatic-water recipe for third-party lyophilised vials. No reconstitution volume, syringe-unit conversion or post-reconstitution lifetime is provided.</p></div>
      </section>

      <section id="regimens">
        <p class="eyebrow">D · Studied regimens and duration</p><h2>Study design is not a use protocol.</h2>
        <h3>13. Dosage — studied regimens</h3><p>There is no approved retatrutide dose at the cutoff. Human trials investigated multiple once-weekly dose levels and escalation schemes, including phase 2 obesity arms from <strong>1 mg to 12 mg weekly</strong> and TRANSCEND-T2D-1 maintenance arms of <strong>4 mg, 9 mg and 12 mg weekly</strong>. These are protocol-controlled experimental regimens, not PeptiBloom dosing recommendations.</p>
        <h3>14. Frequency</h3><p>Once-weekly subcutaneous administration is used consistently in the clinical programme. It remains an investigational study schedule.</p>
        <h3>15. Duration / “cycle”</h3><p>Peer-reviewed trials in the accepted corpus include protocol periods of approximately <strong>12, 36, 40 and 48 weeks</strong>. A scientifically validated self-directed “cycle” has not been established.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="interactions">
        <p class="eyebrow">E · Combinations, synergies and interactions</p><h2>Do not confuse triple agonism with stacking.</h2>
        <h3>16. Frequent combinations</h3><p>No evidence-based PeptiBloom stacking protocol is supported. Some phase 3 programmes study participants receiving background metabolic therapies; those designs answer research questions and are not combination recommendations.</p>
        <h3>17. Synergies</h3><p>The principal demonstrated pharmacologic combination is internal to the molecule: <strong>GIPR + GLP-1R + GCGR agonism</strong>. This is not equivalent to combining retatrutide with another peptide or incretin drug.</p>
        <h3>18. Interactions</h3><p>The complete drug-drug interaction profile remains incompletely mapped in the reviewed corpus. Interaction guidance remains incomplete rather than inferred.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="safety">
        <p class="eyebrow">F · Safety and monitoring</p><h2>Observed safety signals, with their limits.</h2>
        <h3>19. Possible adverse effects</h3><p>Across reviewed human trials, gastrointestinal events were the most consistently reported adverse effects, including <strong>nausea, diarrhoea, vomiting and constipation</strong>. Other observations include transient/dose-related heart-rate increases in phase 2 obesity, one serious acute pancreatitis event in that trial, asymptomatic pancreatic-enzyme elevations and altered/enhanced skin sensation or hyperesthesia in a minority of participants.</p><p>Long-term rare-event safety remains incompletely characterized.</p>
        <h3>20. Contraindications</h3><p>No approved retatrutide prescribing label exists at the cutoff, so PeptiBloom does not create an official contraindication list by copying one from another incretin medicine. Evidence gaps remain for pregnancy/lactation, paediatric use and several special populations.</p>
        <h3>21. Parameters studied for monitoring</h3><p>Trials monitor body weight, HbA1c/glucose, adverse events, vital signs and laboratory measures relevant to their objectives. These observations do not establish a universal PeptiBloom monitoring prescription.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="interpretation">
        <p class="eyebrow">G · Interpretation and reader guidance</p><h2>Questions the evidence can — and cannot — answer.</h2>
        <h3>22. Frequently asked questions</h3>
        <details><summary>Is retatrutide approved?</summary><p>No at the 2026-09-03 evidence cutoff. It remains investigational.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Has it produced large weight reductions in human trials?</summary><p>Yes, within studied populations and durations. In the peer-reviewed phase 2 obesity trial, the 12 mg study arm reported a mean <strong>24.2% reduction from baseline at 48 weeks</strong>, compared with <strong>2.1% with placebo</strong>. This is a group-level trial result, not an individual guarantee.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is it proven better than tirzepatide?</summary><p>Not at the cutoff. TRIUMPH-5 is designed as a direct phase 3 comparison, but accepted comparative results are not available in this review.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Does it reverse fatty liver disease?</summary><p>A small phase 2a MASLD substudy demonstrated large MRI-measured reductions in liver fat. That is not equivalent to demonstrated fibrosis reversal, prevention of cirrhosis or established long-term disease modification.</p></details>
        <details><summary>Is long-term cardiovascular protection proven?</summary><p>No. TRIUMPH-Outcomes is designed to investigate cardiovascular and kidney outcomes; no accepted results were available at the cutoff.</p></details>
        <h3>23. Common interpretation errors</h3><ol><li>Calling retatrutide “GLP-3”.</li><li>Treating trial doses as approved doses.</li><li>Equating sponsor topline results with peer-reviewed primary evidence.</li><li>Generalising group-level weight loss to every individual.</li><li>Equating liver-fat reduction with fibrosis reversal.</li><li>Treating risk-marker improvement as proof of cardiovascular-event reduction.</li><li>Claiming superiority before accepted head-to-head results.</li><li>Calling vendor reconstitution/storage directions clinical evidence.</li><li>Treating absence of a rare event in a finite trial as proof of no risk.</li><li>Presenting preclinical or mechanistic findings as demonstrated human outcomes.</li></ol>
        <h3>24. Scientific evidence level</h3><p><strong>Human glycaemic and weight efficacy:</strong> solid within studied scope. <strong>MASLD:</strong> promising but limited. <strong>Long-term outcomes and rare-event safety:</strong> unresolved. <strong>Approved-use evidence:</strong> none. <strong>Third-party preparation and stability:</strong> insufficient.</p>
      </section>

      <section id="references">
        <p class="eyebrow">H · Provenance</p><h2>25. Core references</h2><ol class="references">{references.map((reference) => <li>{reference}</li>)}</ol>
      </section>

      <section id="recommendation" class="recommendation">
        <p class="eyebrow">I · Recomendación PeptiBloom — interpretación de la evidencia</p><h2>What follows from the evidence — and what does not.</h2>
        <p>Retatrutide has a well-supported triple GIPR/GLP-1R/GCGR pharmacologic identity and a substantial human clinical programme. Randomized trials replicate glycaemic and weight signals, and the evidence base now includes peer-reviewed phase 3 T2D data.</p>
        <p>Important uncertainties remain around long-term cardiovascular and kidney outcomes, rare-event safety, durability after discontinuation, direct comparative superiority, special populations and whether the strong MASLD liver-fat signal translates into hard liver outcomes.</p>
        <p><strong>PeptiBloom will not infer</strong> an approved or “optimal” dose, self-directed titration/cycle, universal third-party-vial reconstitution protocol, unsupported post-reconstitution lifetime, stacking recommendation, proven cardiovascular/renal protection from surrogate improvements, fibrosis reversal from liver-fat reduction, or comparative superiority before direct results are accepted.</p>
        <div class="recommendation__bottom"><p class="surface-kicker">Bottom line</p><p><strong>Retatrutide is one of the more scientifically mature investigational multi-receptor metabolic peptides in the current research landscape, with strong short-to-medium-term human evidence for glycaemic and weight outcomes within studied populations. That maturity does not make it an approved medicine or close the major unresolved questions.</strong></p></div>
      </section>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

